      "description": "Watch Napoli vs Arsenal highlights and goals from UEFA Champions League on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on September 9, 2026. The UEFA Champions League took center stage on 2026-09-09 as SSC Napoli and Arsenal faced off in a highly anticipated group stage encounter. With both teams seeking to establish their dominance in the competition, the tactical battle on the pitch promised high drama from the opening whistle. Tactical Discipline and Early Openings Arsenal started the match with clear intent, dictating the tempo through Declan Rice and Mikel Merino . The first major spark of quality arrived in the 13th minute when Bukayo Saka unleashed a stunning bicycle kick that seemed destined for the back of the net, only for Alex Meret to produce a world-class save. Minutes later, Merino tested the keeper again with a powerful header, but the Italian shot-stopper remained firm. SSC Napoli focused on defensive solidity, absorbing pressure and forcing Arsenal into wide areas. The home side's discipline was tested as the first half progressed, with Billy Gilmour receiving a yellow card in the 42nd minute for a professional foul to halt a dangerous counter-attack. Despite the visitors' 58% possession, the score remained level as both teams headed into the tunnel at the interval. The Breakthrough and Substitutions The second half saw a series of tactical adjustments from both benches. Mikel Arteta introduced Christos Tzolis and Bruno Guimarães , a move that would ultimately change the course of the match. In the 75th minute, Tzolis found space and laid the ball off to Martin Ødegaard . The Norwegian playmaker took a touch and rifled a brilliant left-footed strike from outside the box, leaving Meret with no chance and giving the Gunners the lead. In response, Napoli threw on Lorenzo Lucca and David Neres to inject some physical presence into their frontline. The intensity increased as Lucca was booked for an elbow on Ezri Konsa in the 85th minute. Arsenal's defense, bolstered by the introduction of Martin Zubimendi , stood tall against the late onslaught. Late Drama and the Final Whistle Four minutes of added time brought a flurry of incidents. Martin Ødegaard picked up a yellow card for kicking the ball away as tensions boiled over, and Kai Havertz had a late strike ruled out for offside in the 94th minute. Napoli pushed forward until the very last second, but David Raya remained composed to claim high balls and secure the clean sheet. The final whistle confirmed a hard-fought victory for the English side in a night defined by tactical precision and one moment of magic."
